sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch06-15> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Because rule 90 is additive, its patterns are superpositions of the single-cell nested pattern, so it cannot generate randomness intrinsically; any randomness it shows comes from the initial conditions, and deviations such as a low initial density persist forever, unlike in rule 22.";
    dc:type "mathematical";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Additive rule 90 only transmits randomness from its initial conditions";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 6, “Starting from Randomness”; section “Randomness in Class 3 Systems” (from p. 261), p. 265. Scope: additive cellular automata (rule 90) versus non-additive class 3 rules (rule 22)"
}
